# SRG-APP-000211-WSR-000031  -  CAT II
# Anonymous user access to the web server application directories must be
# prohibited.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000211-WSR-000031
group:           V-206394
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-001082
control_rev5:    SC-2

contribution:    Splunk Enterprise requires an authenticated session on its REST endpoints
                 and gates each custom endpoint on a named capability and, optionally, a
                 source address list, so no anonymous write path to configuration or app
                 content exists through the product's own interfaces.

governs:         restmap.conf - The management interface endpoints, and which clients may reach them.

check:           restmap.conf [<rest endpoint name>:<description>] requireAuthentication not_equals false

external:        Anonymous access introduced by a fronting reverse proxy or load balancer
                 that terminates or supplies authentication - network component
                 configuration
external:        File system permissions on app and configuration directories, which govern
                 non-application access paths - operating system
external:        Any anonymous access path exposed by a custom app's own endpoints outside
                 restmap.conf control - application development

citation:        restmap.conf.spec:81-84 (requireAuthentication = <boolean>, "Determines if
                 this endpoint requires authentication", default true);
                 restmap.conf.spec:111-118 and :303-310 (capability /
                 capability.<post|delete|get|put>); restmap.conf.spec:121-127 (acceptFrom)
finding:         A REST endpoint stanza sets requireAuthentication to false, allowing
                 unauthenticated callers to reach an application endpoint, so the
                 application does not prohibit anonymous access to the extent the product
                 allows.
